# Heimdall web UI efficiency

Cautionary example: **Discovery Approve** used to post every `Edits[*]` field via SaveAll (hit `FormOptions.ValueCountLimit` → HTTP 400), then redirect into a full GET that re-ran catalog backfill. Prefer the fixed pattern: tiny AJAX/handler POSTs, disable SaveAll fields until Edit, skip backfill after micro-actions.

## Do

- Prefer **small targeted POSTs** or `fetch` for row actions (Approve, Set, Move, SaveName) — send only `id` / selected names, not the whole table.
- After a micro-action POST, **skip expensive work** on the following GET (TempData flags, `skipBackfill`, skip discovery-gap scans). AJAX that returns JSON should not rebuild the page at all.
- Keep giant edit forms’ fields **`disabled` until Edit** so accidental/action posts omit them (ASP.NET default `FormOptions.ValueCountLimit` is 1024).
- **Scope DB queries** to the rows being touched (`Where(names.Contains…)`, team members only) — never load full tables to update one item (`AssignGroups` / AssignMachines lessons).
- On list pages, weigh **query cost** of status banners (discovery-key scans, full ProcessRuns loads). Show banners; don’t auto-run backfill/upsert on every GET.

## Don’t

- Nest row actions inside a SaveAll-sized form / `formaction` that posts the entire page.
- Auto-`BackfillFromDiscoveriesAsync` (or equivalent full upsert) on every list GET “because something might be missing.”
- Call `LoadAsync()` before a redirect-only POST success path.

## Reference

- Discovery: `OnPostApproveAsync` + AJAX / `SkipDiscoveryBackfill`
- App Lists: explicit **Backfill Discovered**; skip discovery-gap scan on machine-focus GETs
